Parallel worker startup and asynchronous interrupts
===================================================

Run ./maxima-local --no-init --batch-string='run_testsuite(tests=["rtest_parallel_lifecycle"]);'
The existing lifecycle file gains 31 cases, for 526 in total. Run this
fixture in an otherwise idle Maxima process: it temporarily wraps the shared
worker primitives and models unrelated budget reservations.

A controller sends a real runtime interrupt after worker budget is acquired,
after the first thread is created, or after the second thread is created.
Callbacks signal a distinct error or throw to a private parent tag. Per-thread
records separately observe creation, completion and joining before the
fixture performs any emergency cleanup. The full reservation must remain held
until the registered workers have joined, and the original count must return.
The test never joins a completed CCL process twice.

Eighteen explicit transitions cover varied item counts and worker limits;
a fixed-seed 64-case property also varies existing budget reservations.
A separate body test forces both caller and worker participation and requires
actual interrupt delivery to both before checking two exact integer results.
Five portable controls check normal, disabled, nested and empty execution;
another probe runs startup from a real worker instead of the session thread.
Sixty-four queued-callback cases retain an outer disabled policy, and ten
collections must complete while the caller is inside a startup mask.
SBCL and CCL run the native tests. ECL 21.2.1 uses serial scheduling because
its native interrupt deferral is unreliable; a separate regression requests
eight threads, forbids spawning, and checks three exact integer results.
ECL 21.2.1, CLISP and threadless builds retain the portable policy/mathematical
checks. Other ECL versions retain the existing backend but were not validated
here. Native tests are explicitly gated by the scheduler's availability.

The correction defers interrupts only while recording a reservation or a
new thread handle. Mathematical evaluation and worker joining are outside
these new defer scopes, and an outer disabled policy remains disabled.
ECL 21.2.1 can dispatch a queued callback from a C interrupt guard while
Lisp interrupts are disabled. Its contended GET-LOCK also enables interrupts.
An attempted native signal-mask workaround passed narrow probes but hung
existing lifecycle tests, and was rejected. The version-specific serial
fallback preserves mathematical behavior without installing that workaround.

Scope limits
------------
This verifies ownership during startup. Interrupted joins on SBCL/ECL and
cancelling a whole region or an individual long-running calculation remain
separate work under issue #35. CCL already defers interrupts in cleanup.
These runtime callback tests do not execute keyboard handling or the Windows
shared-memory signal transport. No mathematical algorithm or old expected
answer changes, no arbitrary thread termination, and no masking of an
unbounded join are part of this startup change.
